WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:10.480
Hello everybody my name's Lloyd Esler I've&nbsp;
always had a bit of an interest in cemeteries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:18.560
dead things and interesting stories around&nbsp;
early Southland and if you want to come along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:26.400
to the eastern cemetery 6.30pm on the 4th of&nbsp;
November having a bit of a walk around here for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.480
about an hour and looking at some of the&nbsp;
interesting graves and a few of the stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.880
from some of the Southland pioneers. So everybody&nbsp;
welcome to come along to that we're just standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:47.440
beside a grave here this is the McNab grave and&nbsp;
uh one of the early ones here so it's Alexander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:54.800
and Janet McNab but the the um this is the tomb&nbsp;
also of Robert McNab who is a member of parliament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:03.280
and uh well known as the author of a couple of&nbsp;
very important books on Southland one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:12.320
is The Old Whaling Days and Murihiku which was&nbsp;
very valuable bit of early research. Robert was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:19.120
survivor of a family he was the oldest&nbsp;
child while he was away at school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:27.360
um this is in 1871 all his siblings died they&nbsp;
caught a disease and within a few days there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.840
there's four or five children all&nbsp;
died so he ended up being the only child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.640
one of the tragedies of the early years but&nbsp;
not particularly uncommon because medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.120
practice was a bit primitive there&nbsp;
were lots of dangerous things a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.760
of people got drowned in those days and&nbsp;
of course there was infectious diseases so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:59.465
um glad we got one survivor out of the McNab&nbsp;
family to make a contribution to Southland history

